# The toolchain scripts/make-reports.py needs, beyond requirements.in.
#
# It is separate from requirements.in because none of it is needed to build the
# font -- and separate from requirements-test.in because that file declares
# fontbakery and gftools[qa], whose dependency closure drags in pycairo and
# fails to build without the cairo system library. What follows is the subset
# the report generators actually import or shell out to.
#
# Why this file exists: none of these were ever installed, there was no target
# that would have installed them, and nothing said which reports they belonged
# to. The suite fell three releases behind and each missing tool had to be
# rediscovered from a traceback. See docs/REGENERATING-REPORTS.md.

hyperglot>=0.8.1        # hyperglot.html -- note 0.8 moved yaml output from -o to -y
shaperglot>=1.2.1       # shaperglot.html
collidoscope>=0.6.5     # collidoscope.html (imported, no CLI)
fontaine>=1.4.0         # provides the pyfontaine CLI -> pyfontaine.html
Markdown>=3.10          # bibliography.html
diffenator2>=0.0.7      # gftools-proof/

# diffenator2 still imports pkg_resources, which setuptools removed in 81.
setuptools<81

# PyICU is pyfontaine's hard dependency and builds against a system ICU. On
# macOS that is the brewed icu4c, which is keg-only -- `make reports-venv`
# exports PKG_CONFIG_PATH and PATH for it. Without those the wheel build fails
# on missing headers, and the failure names pycairo-style metadata errors that
# do not mention ICU.
PyICU

# Not on PyPI under any name; designspace-problems.html needs it.
designspaceProblems @ git+https://github.com/LettError/DesignspaceProblems

# designspaceProblems imports ufoProcessor at module scope but declares no
# dependency on it, so installing it alone leaves designspace-problems.html
# failing on ModuleNotFoundError.
ufoProcessor

# fontTools' interpolatability check refuses to run without an assignment
# solver: "Install Python module 'munkres' or 'scipy >= 0.17.0'". scipy is the
# one fontTools prefers; munkres is the fallback and is kept because it is tiny.
scipy
munkres

# interpolatable.html needs >= 4.63 specifically. 4.60.1 crashes on this font
# with "expected a nonnegative input, got -7.1e-15": a degenerate contour makes
# the minor eigenvalue come out as a floating-point epsilon below zero, and
# 4.63 added the `if lambda2 < 0: lambda2 = 0` clamp before the sqrt.
#
# Nothing here caps fontTools -- every dependency asks for a lower bound only
# -- so pip resolving to 4.60.1 was incidental rather than a real pin, and this
# line makes it stick. Note this is venv-reports, which is deliberately NOT the
# venv the font is built with; see the comment on `reports-venv` in the Makefile.
fontTools>=4.63

# NOT PIP-INSTALLABLE. Neither is a Python package, so neither can be pinned
# above; both are recorded here because this is where someone will look.
#
#   fontquant -- fontquant.html. Rewritten in Rust upstream, not on PyPI and
#                with no published release, so build the CLI once:
#
#                    brew install rust
#                    cargo install --git https://github.com/googlefonts/fontquant fontquant
#
#                It lands in ~/.cargo/bin, which `make reports` puts on PATH;
#                make-reports.py also resolves it via shutil.which and honours
#                $FONTQUANT. Verified 2026-08-31 against v0.1.3: the rewrite
#                kept the -l/--location flag and the "  key: Type(value)"
#                output the report parses, so no parser change was needed.
#
#   fontcompare -- machine-readability-fonts.html. This one is deliberately NOT
#                regenerated on a version bump. It is a cross-font OCR/VLM
#                benchmark scoring Buena against five other monospaces, needing
#                ollama and a local vision model, and its scores are not
#                reproducible run to run -- re-running it introduces noise that
#                reads like a regression. A v1.228 result remains a true
#                statement about how Buena compares. Refresh it deliberately:
#
#                    bash scripts/fontcompare/fetch_fonts.sh
#                    python -m scripts.fontcompare.run
